   124	
   125	## P3. Routing cannot win under nonpositive System-2 advantage
   126	
   127	**Proposition 3** (one-step routing model). Fix a state distribution ρ,
   128	a System-1 action map `a₁(s)`, a System-2 action map `a₂(s)`, and a
   129	per-call cost `c ≥ 0`. A router is a measurable set `S₂` of states on
   130	which System-2 is consulted. With matched one-step continuation, define
   131	the per-state net advantage `A(s) = Q(s, a₂(s)) − Q(s, a₁(s))`. Then the
   132	router's value over never routing is
   133	
   134	```
   135	V(S₂) = E[ 1{s ∈ S₂} · A(s) ] − c · ρ(S₂).
   136	```
   137	
   138	If `A(s) ≤ 0` ρ-almost surely, then `V(S₂) ≤ 0` for **every** router —
   139	oracle or learned, confidence-based or not — with strict inequality
   140	unless `S₂` avoids all states where `A(s) < 0`.
   141	
   142	**Proof.** Both terms are nonpositive term-by-term. ∎
   143	
   144	**Corollary (frontier degeneracy).** On a panel where the System-2's
   145	per-state advantage is nonpositive, no routing design can recover value;
   146	the cost frontier is degenerate at "System-1 only". Routing research on
   147	such a panel can only lose.
   148	
   149	**Empirical reading.** The 10-seed EXP-RQ3 sweep measured eight
   150	LLM-calling arms (always-LLM, four confidence thresholds, three random
   151	rates); none beat `rf` with a CI excluding zero on any family, and
   152	always-LLM lost detectably on three families. Under the one-step model
   153	this pattern is CONSISTENT WITH `A(s) ≤ 0` across visited states — but
   154	the sweep measures aggregate routing outcomes and cannot establish the
   155	pointwise premise directly; `A(s) ≤ 0` remains an unmeasured premise
   156	that, if true, explains the outcomes (the deterministic schedule control
   157	makes it the parsimonious reading), not a quantity the sweep estimated. Two supplementary observations fit: (a) the
   158	calibrated RF softmax maximum rarely exceeds 0.25 with m = 8 (near the
   159	uniform 0.125), so confidence thresholds τ ≥ 0.25 all induce the *same*
   160	router (route-always); the tested router family collapses to its two
   161	extremes, and the random-rate arms confirm the interior points also lose.
   162	(b) The deterministic schedule control (below) shows the LLM's decisions
   163	are dominated even by a fixed two-phase schedule, reinforcing `A ≤ 0`.
   164	
   165	**Caveat.** `V` is additive only in the one-step model with matched
   166	continuation. Sequential composition breaks additivity (Phase 8.6F), so
   167	the closed-loop statement is verified empirically per-arm rather than
   168	deduced; P3 explains *why* the sweep came out as it did and predicts the
   169	same outcome for any untested router on this panel.
   170	
   171	---
   172	
   173	## L1. Random forests can represent axis-aligned threshold rules; exact imitation is then an empirical observation
   174	
   175	**Lemma 1 (representability).** Let the target rule `r(s)` be a function of
   176	a subset of axis-aligned features (e.g., `stagnation_steps`,
   177	remaining-budget ratio). Suppose training pairs `(features(sᵢ), r(sᵢ))`
   178	are noiseless and cover the rule's decision cells, and there is **no
   179	aliasing**: equal feature vectors imply equal rule actions. Then there
   180	EXIST CART trees (depth ≥ number of thresholds, one regressor per action
   181	on indicator targets) whose ensemble reproduces `r` exactly:
   182	`argmax_a f̂_a(features(s)) = r(s)` on covered states.
   183	
   184	**Scope caveat (added per codex round 5).** This is a representability
   185	statement, not a guarantee about the TRAINED forest: CART fitting with
   186	random subsetting may grow trees that split elsewhere, and coverage plus
   187	no-aliasing do not by themselves ensure the correct partitions dominate
   188	the ensemble average. The claim that a fitted forest DOES recover the
   189	rule exactly is an empirical observation (below), for which the lemma
   190	supplies the explanation of possibility.

